# BUILD: GLOBAL FLAG ARENA — A CINEMATIC, INTERACTIVE YOUTUBE LIVESTREAM SIMULATION

You are acting as a senior creative director, real-time simulation engineer, broadcast graphics designer, audio designer, full-stack engineer and cloud deployment architect.

Build a complete, visually spectacular, interactive **64-country flag battle arena** inspired by the attached reference screenshot.

This is not a static design mockup, a dashboard with a game embedded inside it, or a generic bouncing-ball experiment. It is intended to become a persistent YouTube livestream experience in which viewers can follow countries, anticipate dangerous events, watch actual eliminations, hear country names announced, interact through live chat and receive real event-based acknowledgements.

The reference screenshot is the primary visual direction. Reproduce its overall composition, hierarchy, arena geometry, energy and broadcast atmosphere, but develop original Global Flag Arena branding, interface assets, background artwork and animations. Do not reproduce the reference channel's logo or claim to own its artwork.

## NON-NEGOTIABLE SUCCESS CRITERIA

1. Sixty-four recognizable country flags appear inside a convincing circular physics arena.
2. Country tokens move at a readable speed and collide naturally with the arena boundary, rotating barriers and one another.
3. Eliminations are caused by actual game mechanics rather than scripted outcomes.
4. Every confirmed elimination triggers a visible announcement and genuinely audible spoken commentary naming the eliminated country.
5. Viewers experience anticipation before each hazard event, rather than watching endless chaotic movement.
6. Real YouTube Live Chat events affect the running simulation and produce accurate acknowledgements.
7. Subscriber alerts only claim success when supported by real event data. Unknown, unavailable or private subscriber events must not be fabricated.
8. The application supports a proper horizontal 16:9 broadcast and a separately composed vertical 9:16 broadcast.
9. The application can be deployed to Google Cloud, with a documented, technically valid route to sending the rendered audio/video stream to YouTube Live.
10. The finished result must be tested in a running application. Passing unit tests alone is not sufficient.

Do not claim a feature is working merely because a placeholder, button, mock API response or simulated event exists.

