# 10. TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE

Build this as a maintainable full-stack application, not a monolithic file.

Preferred initial stack:

- React and TypeScript for the application interface.
- Phaser 3 with Matter.js for the real-time 2D arena and physics.
- Node.js server-side runtime for YouTube API access, OAuth handling, speech generation and event processing.
- Firebase/Firestore or another suitable persistent database for session history, settings, event deduplication and stored records.
- Server-side Gemini API integration for selected contextual commentary and TTS where supported.
- A clear adapter boundary around the YouTube API, speech provider and stream output.

Separate the following subsystems:

1. Arena simulation.
2. Game state and match lifecycle.
3. Broadcast rendering.
4. YouTube authentication and API ingestion.
5. Event queue and event deduplication.
6. Commentary text generation.
7. Text-to-speech and audio mixing.
8. Session persistence and standings.
9. Administrative control panel.
10. Deployment and operational health checks.

Use typed event payloads and a single authoritative country roster. Validate external inputs and treat chat messages as untrusted data.

Keep simulation rendering and high-frequency collision processing in the browser or dedicated rendering worker. Do not send every collision to the server or to Gemini. Send only meaningful, rate-limited events to the commentary and integration services.

Provide structured logs, clear connection diagnostics, recoverable errors, sensible timeouts and resource limits. Include tests for core physics, event deduplication, match transitions, persistence and API error handling.

