# 11. CLOUD DEPLOYMENT AND ACTUAL YOUTUBE VIDEO OUTPUT

A deployed web application is not automatically a livestream. Build and document the full path from the simulation to the video and audio signal sent to YouTube.

## Application and event backend

Prepare the main full-stack web app for Google Cloud Run deployment. Store credentials in the appropriate server-side secrets system. Provision the selected persistent database, define required environment variables and provide deployment instructions.

Use HTTPS for the public application and OAuth callback URLs. Do not expose secret configuration to the browser.

Provide a protected operator console with:
- YouTube connection status.
- Active broadcast and chat status.
- Simulation controls.
- Audio controls.
- Match restart and session reset.
- Event queue and last-event information.
- Error and quota diagnostics.
- A preview of the live broadcast layout.

The public broadcast page should not expose administrative functions.

## Video output

Provide two clearly documented output modes.

**Mode A — Browser/OBS output:** Serve the broadcast composition at a dedicated clean URL suitable for capture as an OBS Browser Source. Ensure the arena animation, commentary and sound effects are available in the capture, without administrative UI. Document how to configure OBS and stream the captured output to YouTube.

**Mode B — Cloud-rendered stream:** Prepare a separate, containerized renderer/encoder service that can render continuously in a cloud environment and send the final audio/video signal to YouTube through the supported RTMPS ingestion path. Evaluate an appropriate persistent worker on Google Compute Engine or another suitable deployment target for a continuously running render/encode process. Do not assume a normal Cloud Run web service will automatically generate and transmit a continuous livestream just because the web app has been deployed.

The cloud renderer must support:
- A continuously running simulation.
- Stable frame timing.
- Correct audio/video synchronization.
- Automatic reconnection handling.
- Configurable resolution, frame rate and bitrate.
- Graceful failure reporting and restart behaviour.
- Protected YouTube stream configuration.
- A clean shutdown and restart procedure.

Do not create or start a public live broadcast without explicit operator action. Provide a preflight checklist and a separate explicit Start Streaming action.

If the current AI Studio environment cannot build or run the cloud encoder directly, implement the parts that are supported and deliver the renderer's container specification, configuration schema and exact deployment steps. Clearly identify what has and has not been validated.

Never claim the application is broadcasting to YouTube until a real end-to-end stream test has succeeded.

